1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,742
At 1500 rpm, our 8-pole motor's phase
currents are 100 Hz sine waves:

2
00:00:05,782 --> 00:00:09,597
three moving targets for a current
controller.

3
00:00:09,637 --> 00:00:14,440
In the α–β plane of Lesson 12, they're one
vector, sweeping a circle.

4
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:16,513
Now turn the camera with the rotor.

5
00:00:16,553 --> 00:00:20,251
The circle collapses to a point: two
constant numbers.

6
00:00:20,351 --> 00:00:23,645
That change of viewpoint is the Park
transformation.

7
00:00:24,745 --> 00:00:27,211
First, the answers from Lesson 12.

8
00:00:27,251 --> 00:00:31,372
One: with three sensors, phase a reading
5% high

9
00:00:31,412 --> 00:00:36,194
stretches the locus along α only, to 10.33
by 10 A.

10
00:00:36,234 --> 00:00:40,244
Two: a 0.3 A offset on channel b shifts
the

11
00:00:40,284 --> 00:00:44,506
circle straight up the β axis, by 0.35 A.

12
00:00:44,546 --> 00:00:48,482
Three: a frame turning with the current.
That's today's subject.

13
00:00:49,582 --> 00:00:53,439
Here's a two-pole picture, so all angles
are electrical:

14
00:00:53,479 --> 00:00:57,388
fixed phase axes, and the rotor magnet at
angle θe.

15
00:00:57,428 --> 00:01:01,341
The current vector turns with it, a
quarter turn ahead of the magnet.

16
00:01:01,381 --> 00:01:03,910
Now turn the camera too. As it catches up,

17
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:08,853
one cycle of the trail shrinks from a
circle to an arc, then to a point.

18
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:13,380
The stator axes now spin backward; the
rotor and the current stand still.

19
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:16,222
The rotor's axes: d, the direct axis,

20
00:01:16,262 --> 00:01:21,523
on the magnet's north pole; q, the
quadrature axis, 90° ahead.

21
00:01:22,623 --> 00:01:27,547
Turning the camera forward by θe turns
every vector back by θe:

22
00:01:27,587 --> 00:01:30,468
multiply by e to the minus j θe.

23
00:01:30,508 --> 00:01:37,550
In real numbers, a rotation matrix: i_d is
cos θ i_α plus sin θ i_β;

24
00:01:37,590 --> 00:01:41,908
i_q is minus sin θ i_α plus cos θ i_β.

25
00:01:41,948 --> 00:01:43,668
Its inverse is its transpose:

26
00:01:43,708 --> 00:01:47,467
the drive uses it on the way out, before
inverse Clarke.

27
00:01:47,507 --> 00:01:51,659
Robert Park, at General Electric,
published this in 1929,

28
00:01:51,699 --> 00:01:56,611
for synchronous machines. Conventions
vary; here, q leads d.

29
00:01:57,711 --> 00:02:01,250
Why put d on the magnet? Because then the
magnet's flux,

30
00:02:01,290 --> 00:02:04,654
seen from the windings, is a constant on
d: λm.

31
00:02:04,694 --> 00:02:07,615
Current on d makes a field along the
magnet's axis,

32
00:02:07,655 --> 00:02:10,743
which can't turn it: think of a compass
needle.

33
00:02:10,783 --> 00:02:14,928
Current on q acts at right angles: the
most torque per amp.

34
00:02:14,968 --> 00:02:18,732
Lesson 9 derived it: T is three-halves p
λm i_q.

35
00:02:18,772 --> 00:02:23,844
For our motor, 10 A on q gives 0.39 N·m;
swing that current around,

36
00:02:23,884 --> 00:02:27,906
and torque follows the sine of its angle
from d.

37
00:02:27,946 --> 00:02:31,694
So in a surface-magnet motor, i_q alone
sets the torque,

38
00:02:31,734 --> 00:02:34,737
and drives normally hold i_d at zero.

39
00:02:35,837 --> 00:02:41,476
In steady state, the currents turn exactly
with the rotor, so i_d and i_q are DC.

40
00:02:41,516 --> 00:02:43,546
Watch a start-up at 5 A on q:

41
00:02:43,586 --> 00:02:48,299
the phases show a sine wave speeding up;
dq shows flat lines.

42
00:02:48,339 --> 00:02:52,841
Then the command steps to 10 A: in dq, a
clean step,

43
00:02:52,881 --> 00:02:57,958
with a 0.16 ms time constant for a 1 kHz
current loop.

44
00:02:57,998 --> 00:03:01,717
Integral action tracks a constant with no
steady-state error,

45
00:03:01,757 --> 00:03:05,276
at any speed. That's why current loops run
in dq.

46
00:03:06,376 --> 00:03:13,182
What isn't DC in dq is just as telling:
add 5 % fifth and 3 % seventh harmonic.

47
00:03:13,222 --> 00:03:17,871
As in Lesson 11, the fifth turns backward
at 5 times the frequency,

48
00:03:17,911 --> 00:03:20,440
the seventh forward at 7 times.

49
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:24,793
Multiplying by e to the minus j θe shifts
every line of the

50
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:29,694
complex spectrum down by one: plus one
lands on zero, the DC.

51
00:03:29,734 --> 00:03:34,624
Minus five goes to minus six, and plus
seven to plus six:

52
00:03:34,664 --> 00:03:39,921
both become a 600 Hz ripple, 1.4 A peak to
peak on i_q.

53
00:03:41,021 --> 00:03:43,722
Now Lesson 12's sensor offset:

54
00:03:43,762 --> 00:03:49,146
0.2 A on channel a moved the circle's
centre by 0.23 A.

55
00:03:49,186 --> 00:03:54,443
In the rotating frame, that fixed vector
turns backward once per cycle:

56
00:03:54,483 --> 00:03:59,320
a 0.23 A ripple on i_d and i_q, at the
electrical frequency.

57
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:02,590
Gain mismatch ripples at twice that
frequency instead:

58
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:04,928
the frequency names the culprit.

59
00:04:04,968 --> 00:04:11,072
With the loop closed, the real current
carries the ripple instead:

60
00:04:11,112 --> 00:04:14,964
9 mN·m on 0.39 N·m, 2.3 % at 100 Hz.

61
00:04:16,064 --> 00:04:19,128
All this needs θe, the rotor's electrical
angle.

62
00:04:19,168 --> 00:04:22,050
The most accurate source is an encoder.

63
00:04:22,090 --> 00:04:27,020
Take a 1024-line quadrature encoder: 4096
counts per turn.

64
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:32,562
With 4 pole pairs, multiply the count by 4
and wrap at 4096.

65
00:04:32,602 --> 00:04:37,783
Count 1000, for example, is 351.56
electrical degrees.

66
00:04:37,823 --> 00:04:44,080
One count, 0.35 electrical degrees, is
negligible for torque.

67
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:46,975
An incremental encoder also needs a
reference,

68
00:04:47,015 --> 00:04:50,976
like an index pulse, and an offset that
puts zero on d.

69
00:04:52,076 --> 00:04:54,679
Cheaper drives use three Hall sensors,

70
00:04:54,719 --> 00:04:58,348
which split each cycle into six sectors of
60°.

71
00:04:58,388 --> 00:05:02,019
Without interpolation, the drive knows
only the sector.

72
00:05:02,059 --> 00:05:07,225
Take its centre, and the error is a
sawtooth between plus and minus 30°.

73
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:10,550
Interpolation estimates the speed from the
last sector,

74
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:13,792
and advances the angle smoothly from each
edge.

75
00:05:13,832 --> 00:05:19,782
At steady speed, with ideal sensors, it's
exact; ramping from 300 to

76
00:05:19,822 --> 00:05:26,577
1500 rpm in 0.1 s, it lags up to 11° early
on, and under 1° by the end.

77
00:05:27,699 --> 00:05:32,140
What does an angle error do? Say the
drive's angle runs 10° ahead.

78
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:34,527
Its frame turns with it:

79
00:05:34,567 --> 00:05:41,094
10 A on the true q axis reads as 9.85 A on
q, and 1.74 A on d.

80
00:05:41,134 --> 00:05:47,350
Close the loop, and the controller pulls
the current onto its own q axis:

81
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:51,350
really 9.85 A on q, and minus 1.74 A on d.

82
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:59,453
Torque is 98.5 % of the command: 0.384
instead of 0.39 N·m.

83
00:05:59,493 --> 00:06:04,955
Unaided Hall sensors dip to 86.6 % at
sector edges. And past 90°,

84
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:09,897
torque reverses: the classic sign of a
wrong offset.

85
00:06:10,997 --> 00:06:14,242
The magnet's flux linkage sits on d, so
the back-EMF,

86
00:06:14,282 --> 00:06:17,989
its rate of change, points a quarter turn
ahead: on q.

87
00:06:18,029 --> 00:06:24,191
At constant speed, with a sinusoidal EMF,
e_q is ωe λm,

88
00:06:24,231 --> 00:06:31,470
and e_d is zero: DC again. For our motor,
4.08 V at 1500 rpm.

89
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:34,140
That's a bench check of the encoder
offset:

90
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:38,824
spin the motor from outside, inverter off,
and log two line voltages.

91
00:06:38,864 --> 00:06:43,658
Clarke, then Park with the encoder angle;
trim the offset until

92
00:06:43,698 --> 00:06:49,338
e_d averages zero with e_q positive. Each
degree leaves 71 mV on d.

93
00:06:50,438 --> 00:06:53,760
Now the exercise. A synthetic log: 10 A on
q,

94
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:57,747
harmonics, an offset, noise, and encoder
counts.

95
00:06:57,787 --> 00:07:02,919
Counts become the angle, and one complex
multiplication gives i_d and i_q.

96
00:07:02,959 --> 00:07:07,755
With the encoder angle, i_d averages zero
and i_q 10 A;

97
00:07:07,795 --> 00:07:14,047
the spectrum shows the offset at −100 Hz
and the harmonics at ±600.

98
00:07:14,087 --> 00:07:20,920
Add a deliberate 10° error: i_d moves to
1.74 A, i_q to 9.85.

99
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:28,427
With Hall sectors only, i_q averages 9.6 A
and i_d swings nearly 10 A peak to peak.

100
00:07:29,527 --> 00:07:31,807
Three predictions before next time.

101
00:07:31,847 --> 00:07:34,239
One: with a 10° electrical angle error,

102
00:07:34,279 --> 00:07:37,896
what torque do you get for the same
commanded i_q?

103
00:07:38,796 --> 00:07:43,548
Two: Hall sensors without interpolation.
What's the worst angle error?

104
00:07:44,448 --> 00:07:49,220
Three: at constant speed, what does the
back-EMF look like in dq?

105
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:53,229
Next time: the d–q model, with its voltage
equations,

106
00:07:53,269 --> 00:07:55,967
cross-coupling, and the three-halves.
